24	\begin{abstract}
25	High-mobility cellular vehicle-to-infrastructure (C-V2I) orthogonal frequency-division multiplexing (OFDM) relies on channel prediction to combat channel-state-information (CSI) aging, and the prediction is driven by a receiver-side speed (hence Doppler) estimate that may contain bias. This paper analyzes, on a sum-of-sinusoids Jakes channel, how far such prediction can reach and how a biased speed estimate should be used. We show that, for the one-tap predictor considered here, (i) the bit-error rate (BER) is governed primarily by the prediction horizon relative to the channel coherence time rather than by predictor sophistication, and that a first-order autoregressive (AR(1)) surrogate hides this by overstating temporal correlation (lag-64 correlation $0.98$ versus $0.16$ for sum-of-sinusoids Jakes at 120 km/h); and (ii) because no single pilot period is efficient across the C-V2I speed range, a lightweight receiver that uses its speed estimate to adapt the prediction horizon (equivalently, the pilot period) improves goodput. The proposed coherence-aware rule tracks the goodput envelope over $30$--$210$ km/h, gaining about $16\%$ average goodput over a fixed low-overhead design that collapses at high speed while retaining $O(N)$ per-symbol receiver complexity and adding only an $O(1)$ scheduling update per block. At 120 km/h, a signed-bias sweep shows that goodput varies by about $0.6\%$ under a $\pm20$ km/h speed-estimation bias; a \emph{positive} bias is conservative because it shortens the selected pilot period. The results also caution against validating high-mobility receivers on AR(1) surrogates.
26	\end{abstract}
