32	\section{Introduction}
33	High-mobility C-V2I OFDM links suffer from CSI aging, Doppler spread, and equalization mismatch \cite{nagalapur,choi,ko}. A large body of work improves CSI through pilot-based estimation, temporal prediction, and learning-aided tracking \cite{zhang,talaei,pereira,liu1,huang,penna,liu2,ding,ju}, typically driven by a speed or Doppler estimate that may contain bias from sensor and quantization effects. We treat a positive speed bias as a deliberate stress-test assumption rather than a claim that such estimates are always biased. Two questions are usually left implicit: how far ahead can such a predictor usefully reach on a realistically fading channel, and how should a possibly \emph{biased} speed estimate be used so that detection stays robust?
34	
35	This paper answers both. Working on a sum-of-sinusoids Jakes channel rather than an analytically convenient surrogate, we find that end-to-end BER is dictated by the prediction horizon measured against the coherence time: once the horizon exceeds roughly one coherence time, prediction from the aged pilot estimate no longer recovers the channel in our simulations, and predicting ahead becomes indistinguishable from reusing an aged estimate. This reframes the design problem from ``predict better'' to ``predict only as far as the channel stays coherent.'' Since vehicle speed---and hence coherence time---varies widely in C-V2I, no fixed pilot period is efficient throughout: a short period wastes overhead at low speed, a long one collapses at high speed. We therefore propose a lightweight, coherence-aware receiver that uses its (biased) speed estimate to adapt the prediction horizon, equivalently the pilot period, and we analyze its robustness to speed-estimation bias. Contributions: (i) a sum-of-sinusoids-Jakes characterization of the horizon/coherence limit and a demonstration that AR(1) surrogates hide it; (ii) an $O(N)$ speed-aware scheduling rule that tracks the goodput envelope over the C-V2I speed range; and (iii) a signed Doppler-mismatch analysis at 120 km/h showing stable goodput under $\pm20$ km/h bias, with positive bias identified as the conservative direction.
36	
37	\section{System Model and Problem Formulation}
38	\subsection{OFDM Baseband Model}
39	Consider a single-link high-mobility C-V2I OFDM system with $N$ subcarriers. After cyclic-prefix removal and FFT processing, the received symbol on subcarrier $k$ at OFDM-symbol index $n$ is
40	\begin{equation}
41	Y_k(n)=H_k(n)X_k(n)+I_k(n)+N_k(n),
42	\label{eq:ofdm}
43	\end{equation}
44	where $H_k(n)$ is the channel frequency response, $I_k(n)$ is inter-carrier interference (ICI), and $N_k(n)\sim\mathcal{CN}(0,\sigma_n^2)$. With $N=64$ and $10$-MHz bandwidth, $T_u=6.4~\mu$s; at 120 km/h and 5.9 GHz, $f_D\approx656$ Hz and $f_DT_u\approx0.0042$, so the ICI-to-signal ratio is below $-40$ dB and ICI is absorbed into the effective disturbance. The dominant high-mobility impairment at this operating point is therefore CSI aging across the pilot-to-data gap, not ICI.
45	
46	\subsection{Fading Model and Coherence Time}
47	The frequency response follows from $L$ taps $h_\ell(n)$ as $H_k(n)=\sum_{\ell=0}^{L-1}h_\ell(n)e^{-j2\pi k\ell/N}$. For a vehicle at speed $v$ (expressed in m/s when evaluating the Doppler relation), $f_D=vf_c/c$ and the temporal correlation of sum-of-sinusoids Jakes fading at lag $d$ symbols is
48	\begin{equation}
49	\rho(v,d)=J_0\!\left(2\pi f_D\, d\, T_{\rm sym}\right).
50	\label{eq:jakes}
51	\end{equation}
52	An AR(1) surrogate $h_\ell(n{+}1)=\alpha(v)h_\ell(n)+W_\ell(n)$, $\alpha(v)=J_0(2\pi f_DT_{\rm sym})$, has lag-$d$ correlation $\alpha(v)^d$, which \emph{greatly exceeds} \eqref{eq:jakes} at the horizons of interest. Table~\ref{tab:corr} makes the gap explicit: at 120 km/h the surrogate retains $0.98$ correlation at $d=64$ while sum-of-sinusoids Jakes has decayed to $0.16$. Any conclusion about how far prediction can reach is therefore only as trustworthy as the channel model; we use sum-of-sinusoids Jakes throughout.
