102	\subsection{Design Principle}
103	Section~IV shows that BER tracks the correlation $\rho(v,d)$ at the operative horizon. The receiver therefore chooses the \emph{largest} pilot period whose worst-case horizon still exceeds a target correlation $\rho_{\rm th}$, using its speed estimate:
104	\begin{equation}
105	P^\star(\hat v)=\max\big\{P: J_0(2\pi f_D(\hat v)\,(P{-}1)\,T_{\rm sym})\ge\rho_{\rm th}\big\},
106	\label{eq:rule}
107	\end{equation}
108	clamped to $[2,P_{\max}]$. Higher estimated speed yields a shorter period (denser pilots), lower speed a longer one (less overhead). Within each block the CSI is obtained by one-tap prediction $\widehat H_k(n{+}j)=\alpha(\hat v)^j\widehat H_k(n)$ followed by MMSE equalization. Prediction and equalization retain the conventional $O(N)$ per-symbol complexity, while the scheduling logic adds only one $O(1)$ update per block.
109	
110	For $\rho_{\rm th}$ close to unity the small-argument expansion $J_0(x)\approx1-x^2/4$ inverts \eqref{eq:rule} in closed form. Setting $J_0\big(2\pi f_D(\hat v)(P^\star{-}1)T_{\rm sym}\big)=\rho_{\rm th}$ gives
111	\begin{equation}
112	P^\star(\hat v)\approx 1+\frac{\sqrt{1-\rho_{\rm th}}}{\pi f_D(\hat v)\,T_{\rm sym}}
113	=1+\frac{c\,\sqrt{1-\rho_{\rm th}}}{\pi f_c T_{\rm sym}\,\hat v},
114	\label{eq:closed}
115	\end{equation}
116	so the period scales as $1/\hat v$: it halves when the estimated speed doubles. Equivalently, $P^\star$ tracks the coherence time $T_{\rm coh}\approx0.42/f_D$ (about $645~\mu$s at 120 km/h), holding the pilot-to-data gap $(P^\star{-}1)T_{\rm sym}$ at a fixed fraction of $T_{\rm coh}$. Equation~\eqref{eq:closed} reproduces the simulated selections ($P^\star=20/26/12$ at $120/90/210$ km/h) and reduces the per-block update to an $O(1)$ arithmetic expression with no Bessel evaluation.
117	
118	\subsection{Robustness to Speed Bias}
119	Rule \eqref{eq:rule} is monotone in $\hat v$. A positive bias $b_v>0$ makes $f_D(\hat v)$ too large, hence $P^\star$ too small: the receiver over-provisions pilots and pays a small overhead penalty but reduces the risk of exceeding the coherence-limited horizon---the conservative error. A negative bias lengthens the period and risks operating past coherence. The absolute shift in $P^\star$ is larger at low speeds, where the rule approaches $P_{\max}$, and smaller around the 120-km/h operating point used for the goodput bias sweep (Sec.~IV-D). Quantitatively, differentiating \eqref{eq:closed} gives $\partial P^\star/\partial\hat v=-(P^\star{-}1)/\hat v$, so a fractional speed error $b_v/\hat v$ produces the same fractional change in the gap---at 120 km/h a $+20$ km/h bias shortens the gap by about $17\%$, a benign over-provisioning. This is the precise sense in which positive bias is conservative.
120	
121	\subsection{Complexity and Implementation}
122	Per block the receiver (i) forms $f_D(\hat v)=\hat v f_c/c$, (ii) sets $P^\star$ from the closed form \eqref{eq:closed}, and (iii) for each of the $P^\star{-}1$ data symbols predicts $\widehat H_k(n{+}j)=\alpha(\hat v)^j\widehat H_k(n)$ and applies the one-tap MMSE equalizer $W_k=\widehat H_k^*(n{+}j)/(|\widehat H_k(n{+}j)|^2+\sigma_n^2)$. Table~\ref{tab:complexity} lists the per-symbol operation counts. Prediction and equalization are scalar per subcarrier ($O(N)$), identical to a conventional fixed-period predictor; the only added cost is the $O(1)$ period update once per block. There is no matrix inversion, iteration, or training, so the design is well suited to real-time C-V2I hardware.
