142	\section{Simulation Results and Discussion}
143	\subsection{Setup}
144	All results use sum-of-sinusoids Jakes fading with a 3-tap exponential power-delay profile ($0/{-}3/{-}6$ dB at delays $0/1/2$ samples), $N=64$, $T_{\rm sym}=8~\mu$s, QPSK, comb pilots (4-subcarrier spacing) with LS estimation and linear interpolation, and $\sigma_n^2=10^{-\mathrm{SNR}/10}$ at 20 dB unless noted; $\rho_{\rm th}=0.9$, $P_{\max}=64$, $\sigma_v=3$ km/h. In every frame the receiver draws its speed estimate $\hat v=v+b_v+\nu_v$ per Sec.~II-C, selects the pilot period by \eqref{eq:rule} (fixed-$P$ baselines skip this step but use the same $\hat v$ for prediction), and applies the one-tap predictor $\alpha(\hat v)^j$ followed by MMSE equalization; reported $P^\star$ values refer to rule \eqref{eq:rule} evaluated at the mean estimate. Each Jakes tap is synthesized as a sum of $M_{\rm J}=32$ complex sinusoids with arrival angles $\theta_i=2\pi(i-\tfrac12)/M_{\rm J}$ and independent uniform phases drawn per tap and per frame, approximating the Jakes autocorrelation $J_0(2\pi f_D\,\Delta t)$. Each point averages $5000$ Monte Carlo frames ($3000$ for 16-QAM). Adaptive-rule goodput values are reported with $95\%$ CIs; Table~\ref{tab:results} also summarizes the fixed-scheme CI bound. Table~\ref{tab:params} lists the simulation parameters.
