214	Figure~\ref{fig:Pbias} shows $P^\star$ under unbiased and $\pm20$ km/h biased estimates: a positive bias consistently selects a shorter (more conservative) period, whereas a negative bias selects a longer one. The absolute shift is larger at low speeds, where $P^\star$ approaches $P_{\max}$, and smaller at moderate and high speeds. Figure~\ref{fig:bias-gp} sweeps the signed bias at 120 km/h: goodput stays within $1.814$--$1.825$ bits/symbol across $b_v\in[-20,20]$ km/h---a variation of about $0.6\%$, with the largest dip at $b_v=-20$ km/h. Thus, at this operating point, the rule is insensitive to the tested Doppler mismatch, and a positive bias costs only marginal overhead while reducing the risk of exceeding the coherence-limited horizon.
