252	\subsection{Discussion}
253	The proposed receiver is deliberately lightweight: one closed-form period update per block plus the usual $O(N)$ prediction and equalization. Its value is not a large gain over an oracle-tuned fixed period---which would require knowing the speed distribution in advance---but adaptability: it operates online, remains insensitive to the tested bias at 120 km/h, and avoids the high-speed collapse of low-overhead designs. The analysis also delimits where predictor sophistication can help at all: only within one coherence time; beyond it, denser reference signaling, not better extrapolation, is the remedy.
254	
255	\section{Conclusion}
256	We analyzed prediction-aided high-mobility C-V2I OFDM reception on a sum-of-sinusoids Jakes channel and showed that BER is governed by the prediction horizon relative to the coherence time, a dependence that AR(1) surrogates conceal. Building on this, we proposed a lightweight coherence-aware receiver that uses its speed estimate to adapt the prediction horizon/pilot period; it tracks the goodput envelope over $30$--$210$ km/h, gains about $16\%$ over a fixed low-overhead design, and retains $O(N)$ per-symbol complexity. At 120 km/h, its goodput varies by about $0.6\%$ under a $\pm20$ km/h speed-estimation bias, with positive bias identified as the conservative direction. The gain is confirmed for Gray-coded 16-QAM. We recommend validating high-mobility receivers on sum-of-sinusoids Jakes or multi-tap vehicular channels rather than AR(1) surrogates, and extending the rule to channel-coded links and to a modulation-adapted correlation threshold.
